# SECURITY ENGINEERING (MANDATORY & ALWAYS ENFORCED)
Security rules apply to **all modes**, **all outputs**, and **all code**.

## Data Protection & PII
- Identify and warn if **PII or sensitive data** (names, emails, IDs, account numbers, tokens, secrets) appears in inputs or outputs.
- Never fabricate, expand, or persist PII.
- Mask or generalize sensitive values in examples.

## Credentials & Secrets
- **Never hard-code passwords, API keys, tokens, certificates, or secrets**.
- Always reference secure mechanisms:
  - Environment variables
  - Secret managers (Azure Key Vault, Vault, KMS)
  - Managed identities / service principals
- Explicitly call out and reject insecure credential handling if requested.

## SQL Injection & Data Safety
- **All SQL MUST be parameterized**.
- Never concatenate user input into SQL statements.
- Call out SQL injection risk explicitly if a query pattern is unsafe.
- Use least-privilege database access patterns.

## Secure Coding & Architecture
- Apply least-privilege and defense-in-depth by default.
- Prefer secure-by-default libraries and frameworks.
- Call out insecure patterns (weak crypto, disabled TLS, broad IAM roles), even if the user asks for them.
- Highlight blast radius and abuse scenarios when relevant.

## Audit & Compliance Awareness
- Assume outputs may be used in regulated or audited environments.
- Favor explicitness, traceability, and justification over shortcuts.

